===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|なみだ|y=k|alt=泪,涕}}

From {{inh|ja|ojp|sort=なみだ|-}}. Ultimately from {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|sort=なみだ|*namita}}.

Attested in the ''{{w|Nihon Shoki}}'' with the {{w|man'yōgana}} spelling {{ja-r|那%瀰%多|な%み%た|linkto=-}}. The ''{{w|Kojiki}}'' and ''{{w|Man'yōshū}}'' have {{ja-r|那%美%多|な%み%た|linkto=-}},<ref>{{RQ:Manyoshu|5|798}}</ref> and the ''Man'yōshū'' also has later instances where the {{ja-r|多|た}} was replaced with {{ja-r|太|だ}}, forming {{ja-r|奈%美%太|な%み%だ|linkto=-}},<ref>{{RQ:Manyoshu|20|4398}}</ref> indicating a phonetic change over time from ''namita'' to ''namida''.

Ultimate derivation {{unknown|ja|nocap=1|sort=なみだ}}. There are interesting phonetic and semantic similarities with {{cog|th|น้ำตา||tear|lit=water + eye}}, overlapping semantically with {{cog|ko|눈물||tear|lit=eye + water}}, perhaps pointing to an ancient prehistoric borrowing. {{w|Alexander Vovin}} lists it among "naturalized loanwords from Korean".<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=Vovin, Alexander|authorlink= Alexander Vovin |chapter=3|section=2|volume=1|edition=revised, updated and enlarged 2nd|title=A Descriptive and Comparative Grammar of Western Old Japanese|location=Leiden|publisher={{w|Brill Publishers|Brill}}|year=2020|oclc=1155482997|isbn=978-0-684-16498-4|page=65|url=https://brill.com/display/title/56720}}.</ref> However, such similarities may also be purely accidental.

